CLAUDE.md:89:- **Install/upgrade ownership is ONE decision, not a cascade (PLAN-167, landed `7c0828a`; PLAN-168 closed the follow-ups).** Whether the framework owns `PROTOCOL.md`, `SPEC/v1` or `.claude/.framework-version` is answered by `_ownership_verdict()` in `scripts/_framework_manifest_set.sh` — a pure function of 10 dimensions returning `"<VERDICT> <HASH_SOURCE>"`. `install.sh` and `upgrade.sh` **observe → call → execute**; they do not decide. Contract: `docs/ownership-decision-table.md`. Truth: `scripts/tests/ownership_table.tsv`. Two oracles read that same table — a unit one (`test-ownership-verdict-unit.sh`, milliseconds: catches a wrong DECISION) and an e2e one (`test-ownership-table.sh`, ~25 min of real installs: catches a wrong OBSERVATION), plus the INV-4 e2e (`test-protocol-pointer-inv4.sh`: install and upgrade render the pointer through the ONE shared generator — byte-identical, degraded bodies CURED with backup, adopter edits preserved). CI: the unit oracle + fast controls run per-PR in `smoke-install.yml`; the full e2e runs in `ownership-nightly.yml`, whose gate (`ownership-nightly-gate.sh`) compares the exact RED id set against `ownership-expected-reds.txt` and fails on ANY difference. **The e2e ends 62 green / 3 red by design** (`OWN-0024`/`0027` test defects, `OWN-0016` product — causes in ADR-190; `OWN-0074` was closed by PLAN-168 W2). An all-green run means the table changed — stop and find out why. Adding a branch that decides ownership locally re-opens the class this replaced.
   240	| `HASH_PRIOR_RECORD` | carry the digest the previous manifest recorded |
   241	| `HASH_CANONICAL_POINTER` | the computed hash of what the pointer heredoc *would* generate |
   242	| `HASH_NONE` | emit no record |
   243	| `LINK_RECORD` | emit `LINK  <relpath>  <target>` instead of a digest |
   244	
   245	The two fields are **orthogonal**. `PRESERVE_OWNED` with `HASH_TARGET`
   246	records the adopter's edited bytes as the framework baseline — which is how
   247	a later upgrade comes to overwrite them and `uninstall.sh` comes to delete
   248	them. `PRESERVE_OWNED` with `HASH_PRIOR_RECORD` is the safe reading of the
   249	same intent. Nothing in the branch structure made that choice visible; a
   250	column does.
   251	
   252	### 3.3 What this replaces
   253	
   254	`FMS_HASH_ROOT_PATHS` and `FMS_LINK_PATHS` are per-path override lists added
   255	during the eleven rounds to narrow two global switches that turned out to be
   256	too wide. They are **special cases of an explicit `hash_source`**, and the
   257	implementation replaces them — it does not keep them alongside it. Adding a
   258	third override list is the failure mode this table exists to prevent.
   259	
   260	`FMS_PROTOCOL_HASH` carries **two different meanings** today: the canonical
   261	pointer hash on the upgrade path, and a *prior record digest* on the install
   262	continuity path. Under this model those are `HASH_CANONICAL_POINTER` and
   263	`HASH_PRIOR_RECORD` — distinct values that must not share a channel. See
   264	OQ-4.
   265	
   266	### 3.4 `HASH_TARGET` is the default, and it is never distinctly correct
   267	
   268	Filling the table surfaced something no single branch could show: across all
   269	61 rows, **`HASH_TARGET` is never the right answer.** It appears only as an
   270	`indistinguishable=` annotation on rows where the target was just written
   271	from the framework's own bytes, so the two candidates are equal by
   272	construction and the distinction is unobservable.
   273	
   274	Yet `HASH_TARGET` is precisely what the generator falls back to when no
   275	override is supplied. So the default is right only by coincidence — whenever
   276	target and source agree — and wrong in exactly the situation the override
   277	exists to handle: a preserved adopter edit. Three separate P1 defects are
   278	instances of that one sentence.
   279	
   280	This is an argument for making `hash_source` an explicit, required parameter
   281	of the verdict rather than a set of opt-in overrides on a permissive default.
   282	Recorded as evidence for the W1 debate, not decided here.
   283	
   284	## 4. Legality rules (pruning)
   285	
   286	A cell is **illegal** when the combination cannot occur against a real
   287	target. Every rule below removes cells; **each is named with its reason, and
   288	silent pruning is forbidden** — an unexplained absence from the TSV is
   289	indistinguishable from an oversight, which is how a defect class hides.
   290	
   291	| Rule | Statement | Reason |
   292	|---|---|---|
   293	| **R-01** | `operation=install_fresh` ⇒ `prior_record=none` | The manifest is written at the *end* of install. "Fresh" is defined as "no pre-existing manifest", so there is no prior testimony to read. |
   294	| **R-02** | `operation ∈ {install_fresh, install_rerun}` ⇒ `skip_requested=none` | `--skip` is an `upgrade.sh` flag. `install.sh` has no equivalent (verified: zero occurrences). |
   295	| **R-03** | `surface=protocol` ⇒ `source_has=yes` | The pointer is generated from a heredoc, never copied. There is no source file whose absence could be observed. |
   296	| **R-04** | `live_content=legacy_pristine` ⇒ `surface=spec` | The pristine fingerprints are a `SPEC/v1`-tree construct. No equivalent exists, or is needed, for a one-line marker or a generated pointer. |
   297	| **R-04b** | `live_content=degraded` ⇒ `surface=protocol` (PLAN-168 W2) | The degraded template is a pointer construct: only the generated `PROTOCOL.md` ever carried the `{{PROTOCOL_SOURCE}}`-literal body. `SPEC/v1` legacy recognition already has `legacy_pristine`; the marker is a one-line version string with no template to degrade. |
   298	| **R-05** | `live_type=absent` ⇒ `live_content` undefined | Nothing to hash. |
   299	| **R-06** | `skip_requested=descendant` ⇒ `surface=spec` | Only `SPEC/v1` is a tree. A path *under* a single file cannot exist. |
   300	| **R-07** | `live_type=dir_empty` ⇒ `surface=spec` | For the single-file surfaces, an empty directory and a non-empty one behave identically (both yield no record and both are refused as non-regular). The distinction is only load-bearing where per-file records are emitted. |
   301	| **R-08** | `ceremony=user` ⇒ `surface ∈ {spec, protocol}` cannot yield `DELIVER` or `REFRESH` | WS4 guards forbid root surfaces under a user ceremony. **This prunes verdicts, not cells:** those surfaces still legally *appear* under `ceremony=user` as residue of a prior maintainer install, and those residue cells are exactly where two defects lived. |
   302	| **R-09** | `prior_record ∈ {link_match, link_retargeted}` ∧ `live_type ≠ symlink` ⇒ collapse to `link_retargeted` | `readlink` on a non-symlink yields empty, which never equals a recorded non-empty target. Keeping both would be two names for one observable state. |
   303	| **R-10** | Rows are **equivalence classes**, not raw tuples; a dimension the row's outcome does not depend on is written `*` | Forced, not preferred. The raw product is ~24,000 tuples; at the mandated per-cell timeout the suite could not run in a day, so it would not be run — and an unrun suite is worse than a smaller honest one. `*` is the harness's instruction to instantiate the canonical representative, and any dimension that turns out to matter must be split into explicit rows. |
   304	| **R-11** | `live_type=ancestor_symlink` ⇒ `surface ∈ {spec, marker}` | `PROTOCOL.md` sits at the target root, so between `$TARGET` and the leaf there is **no intermediate component** that could be a symlink. The guard is vacuous there, not missing. |
   305	
   306	### 4.2 Conventions carried in the TSV
   307	
   308	- `*` — don't-care, per R-10.
   309	- `-` — not applicable under a rule above (e.g. `live_content` when the
   310	  target is absent).
   311	- `note` may carry structured directives alongside prose:
   312	  `fault=<enum>` (an injected environmental failure),
   313	  `indistinguishable=<enum>` (two `hash_source` candidates that are equal by
   314	  construction on this row — the harness reports `AMBIG`, never a lucky
   315	  green), `invariant=<id>`, `open=<round-id>` (a defect this row asserts and
   316	  the current tree does not yet satisfy).
   317	
   318	### 4.1 Three draft rules, REJECTED with reason
   319	
   320	PLAN-167 §W0.1 offered five pruning rules as "already known". Three of them
   321	are wrong, and adopting them would have deleted cells that hold real
   322	defects. Recording the rejections here so they are not re-proposed.
   323	
   324	- **REJECTED: `operation=install_fresh ⇒ live_type=absent`.**
   325	  A maintainer install onto a target that already carries its own `SPEC/v1`
   326	  is precisely the case ADR-155-AMEND-1 §3 cites for why ceremony-conditional
   327	  enumeration is insufficient. `install_one` EXISTS-skips it, and the
   328	  question of whether the adopter's tree gets inventoried as framework-owned
   329	  is the whole point. The cell is legal and important.
   330	
   331	- **REJECTED: `prior_record=link_* ⇒ mode=link`.**
   332	  `prior_record` describes the previous run; `mode` describes this one. They
   333	  are independent. `mode=link ∧ prior_record=hash` is an open, unfixed defect
   334	  — a `--link` rerun over a copy-installed surface that has since been
   335	  replaced by a symlink, where the absence of a `LINK` row is read as "no
   336	  mismatch" and an arbitrary live symlink is recorded as a trusted delivery.
   337	  `mode=copy ∧ prior_record=link` is a legal re-run after a mode change.
   338	  Pruning either would delete the finding the table exists to hold.
   339	
   340	- **REJECTED: `surface=protocol ⇒ live_type ∈ {absent, regular, symlink}`.**
   341	  Nothing in `_refresh_protocol_pointer` prevents a directory or a FIFO at
   342	  `$TARGET/PROTOCOL.md`. It has neither the non-regular-destination guard nor
   343	  the leaf-symlink guard that `spec` and `marker` both acquired during the
   344	  review rounds — see §5.1. The cell is not illegal; it is **unguarded**, and
   345	  those are opposite things. Pruning it would have converted a live defect
   346	  into an invisible one.
   347	
   348	## 5. Cells whose pair is not obvious
   349	
   350	Only the reasoning lives here; the pairs themselves are in the TSV.
   351	
   352	### 5.1 The `protocol` surface is the family's late sibling
   353	
   354	`spec` and `marker` each acquired, over the review rounds, three guards: a
   355	symlinked-ancestor refusal, a leaf-symlink check validated against the prior
   356	`LINK` record, and a refusal of any destination that exists but is not a
   357	regular file. `protocol` acquired **none of them** — and it is the one
   358	surface written with `cat >`, which follows a leaf symlink out of the target
   359	tree, fails hard on a directory (aborting the whole run under
   360	`set -euo pipefail`, mid-upgrade, after other surfaces have changed), and
   361	blocks forever on a FIFO.
   362	
   363	Two of those three are real gaps. The third is not: R-11 shows the
   364	ancestor guard is **vacuous** for a root-level leaf, so its absence is
   365	correct rather than missing. That correction is itself the point — the same
   366	sweep that found the gaps also stopped a plausible-looking third "gap" from
   367	being patched into existence.
   368	
   369	None of this came from the eleven rounds. It came from asking the question
   370	the table forces: *the same cell, on each surface, must have a declared
   371	answer.* Per PLAN-167 §6 rule 2 it is recorded as rows, not patched
   372	branch-locally.
   373	
   374	### 5.4b The instrument was blind to the damage it was built to detect
   375	
   376	The first version of this harness compared only the target tree. For the
   377	rows where a surface is a symlink, that is not enough: a write that follows
   378	the link lands **outside** the target, on adopter or system data, and the
   379	target itself is unchanged. Such a row could report GREEN while the run
   380	destroyed a file the test never looked at.
   381	
   382	The fixture's foreign file is now a **tripwire**, digested before and after
   383	every run. Any change to it produces status `ESCAPE`, which outranks the
   384	verdict comparison entirely — a row whose pair matches while the run wrote
   385	out of tree has not passed.
   386	
   387	Arming it immediately converted a suspicion into evidence. `OWN-0034` — the
   388	`protocol` surface as a leaf symlink — reports `ESCAPE`: `cat >` follows
   389	the link and writes outside the target. `OWN-0044` (a `spec` symlink, which
   390	is correctly preserved) does not, so the tripwire is not simply firing on
   500	- **INV-2** — `LINK` serialization may cover only paths that were already
   501	  `LINK` records before the run. Otherwise an adopter's own symlink,
   502	  preserved inside an enumerated directory, is promoted into a framework
   503	  delivery record.
   504	- **INV-4** — **install and upgrade must generate byte-identical content for
   505	  the same surface.** Violated today by the root pointer (§5.4e): install
   506	  substitutes its placeholders, upgrade does not. No per-cell ownership
   507	  assertion can see this — the record is correct and the bytes are wrong —
   508	  so it needs an invariant that compares the two writers' output directly.
   509	- **INV-3** — **an execution failure never advances the record.** A caller
   510	  that was handed `DELIVER` or `REFRESH` and could not complete it must
   511	  leave the manifest describing the world as it actually is. This is the
   512	  correction to the C2 proposal below: "the failure inherits the verdict's
   513	  `hash_source`" would record a delivery that did not happen — the framework
   514	  claiming bytes it never wrote, which is precisely the over-claiming
   515	  direction ADR-155-AMEND-1 §3 forbids. The failure path keeps the record
   516	  the surface had *before* the attempt.
   517	
   518	Both are the same shape: a switch that was correct in intent and too wide in
   519	scope. That is the single most common defect class in this space, and it is
   520	invisible to per-cell assertions by construction.
   521	
   522	### 5.2 Ownership continuity and the digest it carries
   523	
   524	Carrying ownership forward across a run that did not write anything is
   525	correct — dropping the record strands a delivered tree as unowned forever.
   526	But the flag and the digest are separate decisions, and four separate
   527	defects came from moving one without the other. A `PRESERVE_OWNED` verdict
   528	therefore always has an explicitly declared `hash_source`, and
   529	`HASH_TARGET` is never its default.
   530	
   531	### 5.3 Deliberate asymmetry: the marker under `--pin`
   532	
   533	Where a pinned pre-v1.3.0 source has no marker, the record is **dropped**
   534	rather than carried forward, so that readers fall back to `VERSION` — which
   535	the pin did update. This is the one place where `OMIT_RECORD` is chosen over
   536	`PRESERVE_OWNED` on purpose, and it is recorded here so it is not "fixed"
   537	into consistency with the other surfaces.
   538	
   539	Its residual is real and is open as OQ-3: `VERSION` in an *external* target
   540	is adopter-owned and never written by the upgrade, so the fallback can still
   541	report a version newer than the content.
   542	
   543	### 5.4 The root `VERSION` file is not a surface
   544	
   545	It is out of the enumeration and out of the upgrade, permanently and
   546	deliberately (ADR-155-AMEND-1 §2). It is named here only so that its absence
   547	from the table is not read as an omission.
   548	
   549	### 5.6 One amendment to the plan's observation contract, and why
   550	
   551	PLAN-167 §W0.3 drafted the observation contract so that `ABORT_SURFACE` was
   552	recognised by *"target unchanged + named warning + rc 0, and **no record**"*.
   553	Implementing it showed that clause is unsound: it makes the verdict field
   554	depend on the `hash_source` field, and §3.2 requires the two to be
   555	orthogonal. Under the draft rule, an `ABORT_SURFACE` that legitimately keeps
   556	a prior record would be observed as `PRESERVE_OWNED` — which is precisely
   557	the distinction OQ-2 exists to settle, silently pre-decided by the
   558	instrument that was supposed to measure it.
   559	
   560	The harness therefore recognises `ABORT_SURFACE` by the **refusal being
   561	emitted at all** — the framework attempted and declined — and reads the
   562	record independently. A refusal leaves no filesystem trace by construction,
   563	so this is matched against a small declared set of operator-visible markers.
   564	That coupling is deliberate and is the reason it is written down: if the
   565	wording changes, these rows fail, and they *should* — the operator-visible
   566	contract is part of what `ABORT_SURFACE` promises.
   567	
   568	The harness also emits `HASH_UNCLASSIFIED` when a recorded digest matches
   569	none of the four candidates. That is never an expected value: it means the
   570	manifest recorded something the model has no name for, which is a gap in the
   571	enum rather than a failing row.
   572	
   573	**`REFRESH` versus `PRESERVE` has no single sound signal.** When the incoming
   574	content equals what is already on disk, a refresh changes no bytes, and three
   575	candidate observables each fail somewhere:
   576	
   577	| Signal | Fails because |
   578	|---|---|
   579	| content digest | an identical-content refresh is invisible to it |
   580	| backup presence | the ADOPTER-FORK *preserve* path also snapshots — a backup proves the framework **looked**, not that it wrote |
   581	| modification time | `install.sh` re-runs placeholder substitution every invocation, rewriting the pointer with identical bytes and a fresh mtime — a write with no semantic content |
   582	
   583	The harness uses mtime, and **only on the upgrade path**, where the forced
   584	route is the one thing that rewrites these surfaces. The boundary is stated
   585	rather than assumed, because each of these signals looked universal until a
   586	row proved otherwise — and two of them were adopted, and withdrawn, during
   587	this work.
   588	
   589	The general lesson is worth more than the mechanism: in a space this shape,
   590	**the instrument needs the same adversarial scrutiny as the subject.** Five
   591	separate fixture defects were found while building this baseline, four of
   592	which produced a confident green or red that meant nothing.
   593	
   594	### 5.7 A guard placed on the right route, reached too late
   595	
   596	The `special`-file rows do not fail where they were expected to. The upgrade
   597	never reaches the SPEC or marker route at all: an earlier stage —
   598	`_emit_deprecation_warnings`, which shells out to a scanner that walks the
   599	whole target tree — opens the FIFO and blocks forever. The run dies before
   600	any surface is refreshed.
   601	
   602	Verified in isolation with both controls: the scanner alone, given a target
   603	whose only anomaly is a FIFO at the marker path, hangs indefinitely; the same
   604	scanner on the same tree with a regular file there exits 0.
   605	
   606	Two consequences, and the second is the more important one:
   607	
   608	1. It is a **new defect of a class the eleven rounds did not reach**. The
   609	   special-file guards were placed on the three routes that *own* the
   610	   surfaces. A tree-walking reader that runs earlier needs the same guard,
   611	   and does not have it.
   612	2. It means the existing guards are **masked**: no end-to-end run can
   613	   demonstrate that the FIFO branch of `_refresh_spec_contract` works,
   614	   because control never arrives there. A green suite would have proven
   615	   nothing about them. The table found this by asserting an outcome the
   616	   route was believed to produce and observing that the process died first.
   617	
   618	Per PLAN-167 §6 rule 2 this is recorded, not patched branch-locally. Per
   619	rule 5 the family sweep it implies is broader than the three surfaces: every
   620	tree-walking reader invoked during an upgrade is in scope.
   621	
   622	### 5.8 A continuity branch that cannot fire
   623	
   624	The symlinked-ancestor guard on `spec` and `marker` ends with a line that
   625	carries ownership forward when the pre-upgrade baseline holds a record. That
   626	line is **unreachable**.
   627	
   628	The relpath sanitizer rejects any record whose path traverses a symlinked
   629	component, and it runs when the manifest is *loaded* — before
   630	`_baseline_has_spec_record` is ever consulted. In the one situation the
   631	continuity line was written for, the record it looks for has already been
   632	removed from the sanitized manifest. The observable outcome is
   633	`OMIT_RECORD`, never the intended `PRESERVE_OWNED`.
   634	
   635	The table therefore expects `OMIT_RECORD` here — but the fix is **not** to
   636	make the branch fire. Making it fire would mean honouring a record whose
   637	path crosses a symlink, which is exactly the provenance fence ADR-155
   638	decision (v) exists to enforce. Under-claiming ownership is the recoverable
   639	direction (ADR-155-AMEND-1 §3). The branch should be **deleted as dead
   640	code**, because a line that promises something it cannot deliver is worse
   641	than no line: it reads, to the next maintainer, like a guarantee.
   642	
   643	## 6. Open questions — the W1 debate agenda
   644	
   645	These are the points where the three input authorities — the eleven review
   646	verdicts, the live branch, and ADR-155/AMEND-1 — do **not** agree. PLAN-167
   647	§W0.1 requires that they be recorded rather than resolved unilaterally.
   648	
   649	- **OQ-1 — Is `ABORT_SURFACE` one verdict or two?**
   650	  A failed backup and an unsupported special file both leave the target
